 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Ozone (O₃) is a gas made of three oxygen atoms. The ozone layer is a thin layer of ozone in the upper atmosphere (the stratosphere), about 15 to 35 km above the Earth.\nRole: it absorbs most of the harmful ultraviolet (UV) rays from the sun, protecting living things.\nDepletion (thinning) of the ozone layer is caused mainly by chemicals released by humans: chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) from old refrigerators, air conditioners and aerosol sprays; halons from some fire extinguishers.\nEffects of more UV reaching the Earth: skin cancer, cataracts (eye damage), weakened immunity, damage to crops and to sea plankton.\nPrevention: the Montreal Protocol (1987) banned most CFCs; use CFC-free fridges and sprays; recycle old fridges properly; protect yourself from strong sun.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Why is the thinning of the ozone layer dangerous for people?",
  "steps": [
   "The ozone layer absorbs harmful ultraviolet rays.",
   "When it is thin, more UV reaches us, causing skin cancer and eye damage."
  ],
  "answer": "More UV rays reach the Earth and cause skin cancer and eye damage.",
  "checks": []
